% Reactivation must survive a binding that makes NEW variables
% reachable.
%
% `p(X)` is stored while X is unbound, so the runtime registers the
% suspension as an observer of X. Binding `X = g(A)` wakes it, but A is
% still unbound and the `fire` rule does not match yet. For the later
% `A = 1` to wake `p` again, X's observers have to have moved onto A at
% the moment X was bound — observation is over *reachable* unbound
% variables, and that set changes exactly then.
%
% `direct/1` runs the same rules with no intermediate variable, so it
% keeps passing even when the transfer is missing: the two goals
% together isolate the reactivation path.

:- module(reactivation_through_binding, [start/1, direct/1]).
:- use_module(library(prelude)).

:- chr_type wrap ---> g(int).
:- chr_constraint p(any), out(any), start(any), gather(any), direct(any).

fire @ p(g(1)) <=> out(1).
gather(R), out(V) <=> R = V.

direct(R) <=> p(g(1)), gather(R).
start(R) <=> p(X), X = g(A), A = 1, gather(R).
